infographic list-column-simple-vertical-arrow
data
  title Five production socket failures, five deliberate defenses
  desc Production socket failures are predictable, not random: each failure mode maps to one defense you ship or pay for at 3 a.m.
  lists
    - label Idle or half-open sockets leak file descriptors
      desc A connected-then-silent client — crashed without a clean close, or a half-open connection whose FIN never arrived — pins an fd forever: no crash, no CPU, no memory growth, just slow accumulation until ulimit -n is hit and accept() fails. Fix — socket.setTimeout(ms) fires 'timeout', then YOU call socket.destroy() to reclaim the descriptor.
    - label Slowloris dribbles bytes to hoard connections
      desc An attacker opens many connections and sends bytes just under any naive idle timeout, exhausting connection capacity with almost no bandwidth. Fix — HTTP headersTimeout and requestTimeout bound the total time to send headers and the request, not just the idle gap between reads.
    - label Unhandled ECONNRESET / EPIPE crashes the whole process
      desc Both surface as socket 'error' events — ECONNRESET is the peer's RST (abrupt close), EPIPE is you writing to an already-closed socket — and one unhandled 'error' throws and kills every other in-flight connection with it. Fix — every socket, server-accepted and client-opened, logs-and-cleans-up in an 'error' listener instead of rethrowing.
    - label Unbounded accept rate exhausts the fd table
      desc Nothing caps concurrent sockets by default, so growth is discovered at 3 a.m. rather than chosen. Fix — server.maxConnections sheds load past the limit (the OS backlog absorbs a few, then refuses) and a deliberately raised ulimit -n makes the ceiling one you picked.
    - label SIGTERM mid-deploy fires a reset storm
      desc Dropping live connections on a deploy or scale-down sends a wave of ECONNRESET to users mid-request. Fix — server.close() stops accepting and lets existing requests finish, then iterate active sockets and destroy() stragglers after a drain deadline, since close() alone waits forever on idle keep-alive.
theme
